    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> charged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1922 both to put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a>'s Jewish national home into effect and to develop self-governing institutions for a population then 78 per cent Muslim Arab.</li><li>The Arab revolt of 1936–1939 cost more than 5,000 Arab, 400 Jewish and 200 British lives; the White Paper of 1939 that followed cut Jewish immigration and land purchase as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> began.</li><li>The UN General Assembly recommended partition on 29 November 1947; the Jewish Agency accepted it, the Palestinian Arab leadership and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> rejected it, and civil war broke out at once.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Of the eleven religious communities the British recognised under the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">millet system</a>, Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and nine Christian denominations, none was Protestant, and since personal status lay with confessional courts there was no civil marriage in Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

