    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration.jpg" alt="Balfour Declaration" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balfour_declaration_unmarked.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">United Kingdom Government signed by Arthur Balfour, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> was a British administrative territory in the region of Palestine from 1920 to 1948. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Arab Revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> broke out in 1916, and British forces drove the Ottomans out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in 1917–1918. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had agreed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-hussein-correspondence">McMahon–Hussein Correspondence</a> to honour Arab independence in the event of a revolt, yet with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> divided Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under the Sykes–Picot Agreement, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> saw as a betrayal; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a> of 1917 it promised support for a Jewish “national home” in Palestine, overriding the emerging principle of self-determination for the existing Palestinian Arab population. At the Mandate's formal start the population was about 11 per cent Jewish, 9 per cent Christian Arab and 78 per cent Muslim Arab.</p><p>In July 1920 a civilian administration under a High Commissioner replaced the military one; the first, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-samuel">Herbert Samuel</a>, a Zionist and recent British cabinet minister, took office on 1 July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> granted Britain the Mandate for Palestine in 1922, charging it to develop representative institutions and to put the Balfour Declaration into effect without prejudicing the civil and religious rights of non-Jewish communities. Arab inhabitants had held the first Palestine Arab Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1919 against the Balfour Declaration; the Jewish community elected its Assembly of Representatives on 19 April 1920.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>'s economy was primarily agricultural and most people lived in the countryside. The 1922 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 757,182 people, 78 per cent Muslim, 11 per cent Jewish and 10 per cent Christian; by 1945 the population was 1,764,520, of whom 1,061,270 were Muslims, 553,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and 135,550 Christians. By official records 367,845 Jews immigrated legally between 1920 and 1945, with another 50,000–60,000 estimated to have come illegally, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a> grew from one-sixth to almost one-third of the population; the Arab increase, larger in absolute numbers, was mostly natural, helped by the end of Ottoman conscription and the campaign against malaria. Jews were mainly urban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> mainly rural.</p><p>Of 26,625,600 dunams, 33 per cent was arable. Much agricultural land stayed with the landowners of Ottoman times, mostly Arab clans and local sheikhs; Jews owned 5.23 per cent of the territory in 1945 and, by Stein's estimate, 7.51 per cent in May 1948, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and the coastal plain, while Arab ownership of arable land averaged 68 per cent of a district. The Land Transfer Regulations of 1940 forbade sales to non-Arabs in the hill country to protect Arab cultivators from landlessness. The Jewish sector of the economy grew 13.2 per cent a year between 1922 and 1947, fed by immigration and foreign capital, the Arab sector 6.5 per cent; by 1936 Jews earned 2.6 times as much as Arabs. Literacy in 1932 was 86 per cent among Jews and 22 per cent among Arabs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jaffa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaffa.jpg" alt="Jaffa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISR-2013-Aerial-Jaffa-Port_of_Jaffa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mandate was held by force, and violence ran through its whole life. Riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in April 1920 killed five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>; in May 1921 almost 100 died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> after clashes between rival Jewish protesters and then Arab attacks on Jews; in August 1929 riots in which 250 people died included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a> massacre of Jews. In 1930 Sheikh Izz ad-Din al-Qassam built the anti-Zionist and anti-British Black Hand, until police killed him near Ya'bad in November 1935. His death fed the Arab general strike of 1936: Jewish farms were destroyed and Jewish civilians killed. After the Arabs rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peel-commission">Peel Commission</a>'s partition the revolt resumed in 1937; the British lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> and Hebron for a time, then crushed it with overwhelming force, 6,000 armed Jewish auxiliary police and Orde Wingate's Special Night Squads raiding Arab villages, while the Irgun attacked Arab marketplaces and buses as “retaliation”. By March 1939 more than 5,000 Arabs, 400 Jews and 200 British were dead.</p><p>From 1944 Lehi and the Irgun turned on the British: Lehi killed Lord Moyne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 6 November 1944, the Irgun blew up the King David Hotel in 1946, killing 92, and hanged two British sergeants after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> prison break; British soldiers killed five Lehi trainees at Ra'anana in November 1947. Over 100,000 British troops were needed. After the UN vote civil war broke out between the communities, and in May 1948 the armies of five Arab states crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colonial-office.jpg" alt="Colonial Office" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foreign_and_India_Offices,_London,_1866_ILN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Illustrated London News, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1922 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> governed through a High Commissioner, with the territory divided into districts under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> Order in Council. The Order provided a Legislative Council of 23: twelve elected, eight of them Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, two Christian Arabs and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, ten appointed, and the High Commissioner. Arabs, 88 per cent of the population, protested at 43 per cent of the seats and at the rule that no ordinance could contradict the Mandate; they boycotted the elections of 1923 and an appointed Advisory Council followed. Britain rejected majority rule, and in 1947 Ernest Bevin admitted it had failed to “secure the development of self-governing institutions” the Mandate required.</p><p>Britain kept the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">millet system</a>, recognising eleven confessional communities whose courts ruled personal status. Samuel appointed Mohammad Amin al-Husseini Grand Mufti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1921, and in 1922 he became president of the Supreme Muslim Council, controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">Waqf</a> and the Islamic courts; the rivalry of his clan with the Nashashibis dominated Palestinian politics. The Jewish Agency, from 1929, ran schools and hospitals, immigration and land purchase and formed the Haganah; it and the Arab Higher Committee grew into parallel governments, an imperium in imperio the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peel-commission">Peel Commission</a> noted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a>'s statement of 1922 said Palestine as a whole was not to become a Jewish national home, a reading the Zionist Executive then accepted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/holocaust"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holocaust.jpg" alt="Holocaust" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selection_on_the_ramp_at_Auschwitz-Birkenau,_1944_(Auschwitz_Album)_1b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernhard Walter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mandate's twenty-eight years remade the land and its two peoples. Jewish institutions built under it carried over into a state: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> school system of 1919, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histadrut">Histadrut</a> of 1920, the Technion in 1924 and the Hebrew University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1925; the Haganah, the Palmach formed with British help in 1942 and veterans of the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> became the core of the Israel Defense Forces. Arab Palestinian attempts to found a higher-education institution failed, which Ilan Pappé attributes to Zionist pressure, British anti-Arab racism and lack of resources. Palestinian Arab society, Rashid Khalidi concludes, was as advanced as any Arab society in the region, though overmatched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a>.</p><p>The Mandate also fixed the terms of a conflict. The British repression of 1936–1939 left the Palestinians without unified leadership, and Ben-Gurion wrote in 1937 that partition would be a first step to “possession of the land as a whole”. The White Paper of 1939 radicalised parts of the Jewish population against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and its quotas, kept after 1945 against American pressure, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> to camps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Its outcome shaped the region: the State of Israel, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> annexed by Transjordan, Gaza under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the 1948 Palestinian expulsion and flight. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> later held that the Palestinian people are entitled to self-determination and a state of their own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haifa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haifa.jpg" alt="Haifa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hanging_Gardens_of_Haifa,_Israel_(50099173503)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ray in Manila, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s position collapsed after 1945. The Anglo-American Committee of 1946 recommended admitting 100,000 refugees and neither an Arab nor a Jewish state; the British kept the ban on immigration. Jewish bombings, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> prison break and the hanging of two sergeants made the Mandate unpopular in Britain, and it referred the question to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. On 29 November 1947 the General Assembly adopted Resolution 181 by 33 to 13, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in favour and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> members against. The Jewish Agency accepted it, though Menachem Begin of the Irgun declared partition illegal; the Palestinian Arab leadership and most of the Arab population rejected it, and the Arab League resolved on a military solution. Britain accepted the plan but refused to enforce it.</p><p>Civil war followed at once and British authority dissolved. The police left Tel Aviv on 16 December 1947, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> was abandoned in April 1948 but for the port enclave, after which the Haganah took the city. On 14 May the last High Commissioner, General Sir Alan Cunningham, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and that afternoon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a> declared the State of Israel, effective at midnight, when the Mandate expired. Troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Transjordan crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>; the war to 1949 left Israel with about 78 per cent of the former Mandate, Transjordan with the annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> and Egypt with the Gaza Strip.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> over Palestine from 1920 to 1948, charged with a Jewish national home and self-government for a population then 88 per cent Arab; Jewish immigration, the Arab revolt of 1936–1939 and the Jewish insurgency of 1944–1948 ended in the UN partition vote, civil war and, on 15 May 1948, the State of Israel and the division of the land.</em></p>

